    "q-186817": {
      "dom_id": "step-q186817",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "$-\\frac{7}{15}+\\frac{3}{5}=${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We put both fractions over a common denominator of $15$ and then add the numerators. This gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{7}{15}+\\frac{3}{5}=-\\frac{7}{15}+\\frac{9}{15}=\\frac{-7+9}{15}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the numerator, we can use either Method 1 or Method 2, shown below.\n\n**Method 1 - Picturing a Number Line**\n\nTo add $9$ to $-7,$ we start at $-7$ on a number line and move $9$ spaces to the right.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14149949/assets/f2d170548e38956afa1354e7df74f645ea5101c3dde6665c110ada01ab7a96b8.png)\n\nSo, $-7+9=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-7+9}{15}=\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - Subtraction in Reverse**\n\nWe can swap the order of the numbers in the numerator, and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{-7+9}{15}=\\frac{9-7}{15}=\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $-\\frac{7}{15}+\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{2}{15}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
